    "logic": {
      "concept": "A trend is not one moving average but 6 of them in agreement. Six EMAs spaced 3 bars apart stack in order when every horizon sees the same direction, and a pullback into that ribbon is where the flow that missed the move re-enters. CCI(10) crossing 0.0 times it: that is the bar the pullback stops being a pullback. The ribbon is the CONDITION and the cross is the EVENT - neither trades alone. Trades are attributed by their ENTRY bar, never the exit, so a move opened in one quadrant that runs into another still scores where it opened.",
      "entry": "LONG when CCI(10) crosses ABOVE 0.0 while the ribbon is aligned bullish; SHORT when it crosses BELOW while the ribbon is aligned bearish. Alignment is the EMAs stacked in order, or - with require_strict_stack=False - the relaxed form: Close beyond the slowest EMA with the ribbon spread on the same side of zero.",
      "exit": "The opposite CCI cross always closes the position. With use_ribbon_exit=True a Close back through the SLOWEST EMA closes it too - that is the request's stop 'anchored just below EMA_24', and it is a SIGNAL because the engine has no stop orders. With use_cci_extreme_exit=False the opposite CCI extreme (+/-100.0) closes it as well. A protective bracket at 2.0 x ATR(14) sits underneath all of them, trailing=False; no ATR take-profit is modelled (tp_atr_mult=None)."
    }
  },
